---> Problem solution:
about system: this is a system to fetch financials data from 500+ crypto-exchanges, blockchains, equity funds etc. 
user load: arround 15000 clients having total 66000 accoutns.
Main problem: 
    user case: balance fetch is critical as no exhcnage provides historical data and for accoutnign and reconcilation we need historical balance, our clients are okay with storing balance in slots like twice a day, or once a day, we call it cutt off timings.
    problem: most of the user were asking for utc 00:00 as cut-off time. [20k + users as per last data i had.]


basic architecture:

job-store (sql db: stores job info like (task_type, user_id, account_id, last_id, last_run_status, retry_count, created_at, updated_at)

task-status-api (.net): 
    - in memory store for having task status for each job (ready:1, running:2, completed: 3, failed: -1)
    - periodically flushes to the job-store to update the status of each job ( every 10 sec).
    - handles duplicate request for same job.
    - it has expiry time for job too ( 3 hrs) to avoid stale jobs in the system.

scheduler (.net):
    - (every minute check for jobs in job-store which need to run and push to kafka broker)
    - picking data from sql views --> push message to kafka topic as bulk action with ordering to make it predictable ---> bulk-publish status to task status-api
    - it was finishing his work within t + 150-200 ms for 20k concurrent jobs.

kafka broker:
    - it has 30 partitions to handle high throughput and parallel processing of messages, with 3 nodes to handle failover and replication.

consumer (.net):
    - picked jobs from kafka topic and start concurrent http requests to data-fetch service , and updating status to task status api.
    - we were using grpc connection to data-fetch service for better performance and lower latency, utilizing multiplexing capabilities of grpc.
    - it was finishing his work within t + 600-700 ms for 20k concurrent jobs.
    - if sucess from datafetch service 
        -> update status to task-status-api as completed.
    - if fails from datafetch service
        -> update status to task-status-api as failed and retry 3 times, if still fails

account api (golang):
    - responsible for disrributing the jobs accross diffrent proxy based on region and configuration users has whitelisted.
    - uses in-memory cache to store api-credentials (fetch from vault services itself).
    - it is able to handle 20k concurrent requests with a response time of p99: 1ms, p100: 40 ms.

datafetch service (python 280 k8 pods, hosted on-premise 5 nodes configuration: [using hyper-v bare metal system with 84 xeon platnium cores, 250 gb ra each):
    -  responsibiliies:
        -> hit : accoutns api and get credientials and configuration[params] and proxy info etc. 
        -> hit redis cache to get market data [with a in-memory cache, fallback to file-based cache, fallback to redis, fallback to fetch from exchange if not found in any cache]
            --> de-compress data, validate data expiry
        -> create ccxt object using these info:
            -> fetch main-account balance from each category from exchanges using aiohttp concurrent http connections [using dns caching, proper timeout and connection pooling for better performance]
            -> send data to aws-s3, and pass a message to aws-rabbitmq for further processing.
            -> fetch sub account balance in second concurrent batches.
            -> send data to aws-s3, and pass a message to aws-rabbitmq for further processing.
        return sucess to consumer service.
    --> it was taking arround t+ 7-8 sec.

data processor service (python: redisrabbitmq consumer):
    - based on message it will fetch specific object from aws-s3 and process it to generate final table data and store it in sql db for further use.
    - if fails retry 3 times and if still fails send to dead letter queue for manual intervention.



